    INSERT INTO mcqs (topic_id, question, options, correct_index, explanation) VALUES
    (v_topic_id, '201: A patient''s blood pressure drops by 25 mmHg systolic and 15 mmHg diastolic when moving from a supine to a standing position, accompanied by dizziness. This defines:', '["Vasovagal syncope", "Orthostatic (postural) hypotension", "Pulsus paradoxus", "Neurocardiogenic syncope"]', 1, 'Orthostatic hypotension is defined as a drop of >20 mmHg systolic or >10 mmHg diastolic within 3 minutes of standing.'),
    (v_topic_id, '202: Which of the following physical exam findings is most specific for diagnosing a right ventricular myocardial infarction?', '["Elevated jugular venous pressure (JVP) with clear lungs", "Bibasilar lung crackles", "S3 gallop at the apex", "Pulsus paradoxus"]', 0, 'An acute RV infarct causes right-sided heart failure (elevated JVP) but spares the left ventricle, meaning blood does not back up into the lungs (clear lungs).'),
    (v_topic_id, '203: A "machine-like," continuous murmur heard best at the left upper sternal border in an infant is pathognomonic for:', '["Ventricular septal defect", "Patent ductus arteriosus (PDA)", "Coarctation of the aorta", "Tetralogy of Fallot"]', 1, 'The continuous, machine-like murmur of a PDA occurs because aortic pressure is higher than pulmonary artery pressure throughout the entire cardiac cycle.'),
    (v_topic_id, '204: When auscultating the carotid arteries, the examiner hears a blowing, swishing sound during systole. This sound is a:', '["Thrill", "Bruit", "Gallop", "Friction rub"]', 1, 'A bruit is an audible vascular sound associated with turbulent blood flow, often indicating partial arterial occlusion (e.g., carotid stenosis).'),
    (v_topic_id, '205: A patient with known mitral stenosis presents with sudden onset of cold, painful, pale, and pulseless left leg. What is the most likely cause?', '["Deep vein thrombosis", "Ruptured Baker''s cyst", "Arterial thromboembolism", "Cellulitis"]', 2, 'Mitral stenosis frequently causes atrial fibrillation, predisposing the patient to form left atrial thrombi which can embolize to peripheral arteries.'),
    (v_topic_id, '206: The "6 P''s" of acute limb ischemia include Pain, Pallor, Pulselessness, Paresthesia, Paralysis, and:', '["Pruritus", "Petechiae", "Poikilothermia (coolness)", "Polycythemia"]', 2, 'Poikilothermia refers to the limb assuming the ambient temperature (feeling cold) due to a lack of warm arterial blood flow.'),
    (v_topic_id, '207: A patient has swelling, erythema, and warmth in the left calf. Measuring the calf circumference 10 cm below the tibial tuberosity reveals the left calf is 4 cm larger than the right. This strongly suggests:', '["Peripheral arterial disease", "Deep vein thrombosis (DVT)", "Lymphedema", "Congestive heart failure"]', 1, 'A unilateral calf swelling of > 3 cm difference is a major criteria (Wells score) indicating a high probability of a DVT.'),
    (v_topic_id, '208: Unlike the edema of DVT or heart failure, the edema caused by chronic lymphedema is classically:', '["Pitting and painful", "Non-pitting and painless", "Associated with a bounding pulse", "Warm and erythematous"]', 1, 'Lymphedema is caused by interstitial accumulation of protein-rich fluid, which initially pits but eventually becomes fibrotic and non-pitting (brawny edema).'),
    (v_topic_id, '209: The examiner deeply palpates the left lower quadrant of the abdomen and then suddenly releases the pressure, causing acute pain in the right lower quadrant. This is a positive:', '["Murphy sign", "Rovsing sign", "Psoas sign", "Kehr sign"]', 1, 'Rovsing''s sign is right lower quadrant pain elicited by deep palpation and release of the left lower quadrant, indicative of appendicitis.'),
    (v_topic_id, '210: A patient with a ruptured spleen complains of severe left shoulder pain. This referred pain is known as:', '["Kehr sign", "Boas sign", "Cullen sign", "Grey Turner sign"]', 0, 'Kehr''s sign is referred pain to the left shoulder due to diaphragmatic irritation (phrenic nerve, C3-C5) from splenic rupture or hemoperitoneum.'),
    (v_topic_id, '211: Referred pain to the right subscapular area is classically associated with inflammation of which organ?', '["Stomach", "Pancreas", "Gallbladder", "Appendix"]', 2, 'Biliary colic and acute cholecystitis classically refer pain to the right shoulder or right subscapular area (Boas sign).'),
    (v_topic_id, '212: Ecchymosis (bruising) around the umbilicus is a sign of retroperitoneal or intra-abdominal bleeding (e.g., from severe hemorrhagic pancreatitis). It is called:', '["Grey Turner sign", "Cullen sign", "Fox sign", "Chvostek sign"]', 1, 'Cullen''s sign is periumbilical ecchymosis. Grey Turner''s sign is ecchymosis on the flanks.'),
    (v_topic_id, '213: A patient has severe epigastric pain radiating directly to the back, relieved by leaning forward. This is the classic presentation of:', '["Acute appendicitis", "Acute cholecystitis", "Acute pancreatitis", "Peptic ulcer perforation"]', 2, 'The pancreas is a retroperitoneal organ; inflammation causes severe epigastric pain that radiates straight through to the back and is somewhat relieved by sitting up and leaning forward.'),
    (v_topic_id, '214: When assessing for hepatomegaly, scratching the abdominal wall while auscultating over the liver to detect the change in sound transmission at the liver edge is called the:', '["Puddle test", "Scratch test", "Fluid wave test", "Ballottement test"]', 1, 'The scratch test uses auscultation to define the liver border, as sound transmits much louder and clearer through the solid liver than through the air-filled bowel.'),
    (v_topic_id, '215: A patient is asked to raise their head and shoulders off the examination table. A midline longitudinal ridge appears in the abdomen. This is:', '["An umbilical hernia", "Diastasis recti", "A Spigelian hernia", "An incisional hernia"]', 1, 'Diastasis recti is a benign separation of the rectus abdominis muscles, common in pregnancy and obesity, not a true hernia because the fascia remains intact.'),
    (v_topic_id, '216: A male patient presents with a bulge in the groin that descends into the scrotum. On examination, the hernia sac passes through the deep inguinal ring, lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels. This is an:', '["Indirect inguinal hernia", "Direct inguinal hernia", "Femoral hernia", "Umbilical hernia"]', 0, 'Indirect inguinal hernias follow the path of the descent of the testes (through the inguinal canal) and are lateral to the inferior epigastric vessels.'),
    (v_topic_id, '217: A hernia that protrudes directly through the posterior wall of the inguinal canal (Hesselbach''s triangle), medial to the inferior epigastric vessels, is a:', '["Indirect inguinal hernia", "Direct inguinal hernia", "Femoral hernia", "Obturator hernia"]', 1, 'Direct inguinal hernias push directly through a weakness in the transversalis fascia in Hesselbach''s triangle.'),
    (v_topic_id, '218: Which type of hernia is more common in women than men and has the highest risk of incarceration and strangulation due to its narrow neck?', '["Direct inguinal hernia", "Indirect inguinal hernia", "Femoral hernia", "Umbilical hernia"]', 2, 'Femoral hernias protrude below the inguinal ligament through the narrow femoral ring, making them highly prone to strangulation.'),
    (v_topic_id, '219: A 6-month-old infant is brought in because of intermittent bouts of severe crying, drawing their legs up to their chest, and passing "currant jelly" stools. The examiner palpates a "sausage-shaped" mass in the right upper quadrant. Diagnosis?', '["Pyloric stenosis", "Intussusception", "Volvulus", "Hirschsprung disease"]', 1, 'This is the classic triad and physical exam finding of intussusception (telescoping of the bowel).'),
    (v_topic_id, '220: A 4-week-old male infant presents with projectile, non-bilious vomiting. On exam, a firm, mobile, "olive-like" mass is palpated in the epigastrium. Diagnosis?', '["Intussusception", "Hypertrophic pyloric stenosis", "Duodenal atresia", "Necrotizing enterocolitis"]', 1, 'The palpable "olive" is the hypertrophied pyloric muscle, causing gastric outlet obstruction and projectile vomiting.'),
    (v_topic_id, '221: During a neurological exam, the patient is asked to close their eyes and identify a familiar object (like a coin or key) placed in their hand. The inability to do so is called:', '["Astereognosis", "Agraphesthesia", "Apraxia", "Anomia"]', 0, 'Stereognosis is the ability to identify objects by touch. Astereognosis indicates a lesion in the sensory cortex (parietal lobe) or dorsal columns.'),
    (v_topic_id, '222: The examiner uses a blunt pen to draw a number "8" on the palm of the patient''s hand while their eyes are closed. The patient cannot identify the number. This is called:', '["Astereognosis", "Agraphesthesia", "Two-point discrimination failure", "Extinction"]', 1, 'Graphesthesia is the ability to recognize writing on the skin purely by the sensation of touch.'),
    (v_topic_id, '223: The examiner touches the patient on the right cheek and left cheek simultaneously. The patient only feels the touch on the right cheek. When touched on the left cheek individually, they feel it normally. This phenomenon is called:', '["Hemianopsia", "Sensory extinction", "Peripheral neuropathy", "Hyperalgesia"]', 1, 'Sensory extinction (or sensory inattention) occurs in contralateral parietal lobe lesions (often right parietal damage causing left-sided extinction).'),
    (v_topic_id, '224: A patient has lost pain and temperature sensation in a "cape-like" distribution over the shoulders and upper extremities, but vibration and proprioception are intact. This specific deficit points to:', '["A complete spinal cord transection", "Brown-Sequard syndrome", "Syringomyelia", "Tabes dorsalis"]', 2, 'Syringomyelia is a cystic cavity (syrinx) within the central spinal cord that selectively destroys the crossing spinothalamic fibers at the anterior white commissure, causing bilateral loss of pain/temperature.'),
    (v_topic_id, '225: A patient suffers a hemisection of the spinal cord on the right side at T10 (Brown-Sequard syndrome). What will the physical exam show below the level of the lesion?', '["Right-sided loss of pain/temperature; Left-sided loss of motor function", "Right-sided loss of motor function and proprioception; Left-sided loss of pain/temperature", "Bilateral loss of motor function; Intact sensation", "Bilateral loss of pain/temperature; Intact motor function"]', 1, 'The corticospinal and dorsal column tracts cross in the medulla, so damage is ipsilateral (right). The spinothalamic tract crosses near its entry in the cord, so damage affects the contralateral side (left).'),
    (v_topic_id, '226: When testing the gag reflex, touching the posterior pharynx tests the afferent (sensory) limb mediated by which nerve?', '["Facial (CN VII)", "Glossopharyngeal (CN IX)", "Vagus (CN X)", "Hypoglossal (CN XII)"]', 1, 'The afferent limb of the gag reflex is CN IX; the efferent (motor) limb that causes the pharyngeal contraction is CN X.'),
    (v_topic_id, '227: A patient has a hoarse voice and a weak cough. Laryngoscopy reveals a paralyzed left vocal cord. This indicates a lesion of the:', '["Right vagus nerve", "Left recurrent laryngeal nerve", "Left superior laryngeal nerve", "Left glossopharyngeal nerve"]', 1, 'The left recurrent laryngeal nerve (a branch of the vagus) loops under the aortic arch and innervates the intrinsic muscles of the larynx. Damage causes vocal cord paralysis and hoarseness.'),
    (v_topic_id, '228: To test the function of the trochlear nerve (CN IV), the examiner should instruct the patient to look:', '["Up and out", "Down and in", "Straight medial", "Straight lateral"]', 1, 'CN IV innervates the superior oblique muscle, which depresses the eye when it is adducted (looking down and in, like looking at the tip of the nose or walking down stairs).'),
    (v_topic_id, '229: An inability to abduct the right eye (look outward laterally) indicates a lesion of the:', '["Right oculomotor nerve (CN III)", "Right trochlear nerve (CN IV)", "Right abducens nerve (CN VI)", "Left abducens nerve (CN VI)"]', 2, 'CN VI innervates the lateral rectus muscle, which abducts the eye.'),
    (v_topic_id, '230: In testing visual fields, a patient has a right homonymous hemianopsia (cannot see the right half of the visual field in either eye). The lesion must be located:', '["In the right optic nerve", "At the optic chiasm", "In the left optic tract or left occipital lobe", "In the right optic tract or right occipital lobe"]', 2, 'A lesion posterior to the optic chiasm on the left side (left optic tract, radiation, or cortex) causes a visual defect in the contralateral (right) visual field of both eyes.'),
    (v_topic_id, '231: During the Weber test, a patient with a normal exam hears the tuning fork:', '["Only in the right ear", "Only in the left ear", "Equally in both ears (midline)", "Not at all"]', 2, 'Normally, the sound is conducted equally through the skull bones to both cochleas and is perceived in the center of the head.'),
    (v_topic_id, '232: A patient presents with acute, severe monoarticular joint pain, swelling, and redness in the first metatarsophalangeal (MTP) joint. This physical finding is termed:', '["Podagra", "Dactylitis", "Tophi", "Heberden node"]', 0, 'Podagra is the classic presentation of acute gout occurring at the first MTP joint (the big toe).'),
    (v_topic_id, '233: A patient has a history of psoriasis. Examination of their hands reveals swelling of the entire digit, making the fingers look like sausages. This is termed:', '["Boutonniere deformity", "Dactylitis", "Swan neck deformity", "Syndactyly"]', 1, 'Dactylitis ("sausage digit") is diffuse inflammation of a finger or toe, characteristic of psoriatic arthritis and other seronegative spondyloarthropathies.'),
    (v_topic_id, '234: A patient presents with a painless, hard, bony outgrowth at the base of the big toe, causing lateral deviation of the toe. This is a:', '["Gouty tophus", "Bunion (Hallux valgus)", "Plantar fibroma", "Rheumatoid nodule"]', 1, 'Hallux valgus is a structural deformity causing the first metatarsal to deviate medially and the great toe to deviate laterally, forming a prominent "bunion."'),
    (v_topic_id, '235: The examiner deeply palpates the anatomical snuffbox. Tenderness here requires immobilization and imaging because it indicates a high risk for:', '["Colles fracture of the radius", "Avascular necrosis of the scaphoid", "Dislocation of the lunate", "Rupture of the extensor pollicis longus"]', 1, 'Scaphoid fractures are notorious for poor blood supply, and missed fractures frequently lead to avascular necrosis and nonunion.'),
    (v_topic_id, '236: The Neer and Hawkins-Kennedy tests are used during the shoulder exam to assess for:', '["Biceps tendon rupture", "Acromioclavicular separation", "Subacromial impingement syndrome", "Glenohumeral dislocation"]', 2, 'Both tests compress the rotator cuff tendons (usually supraspinatus) between the greater tubercle of the humerus and the acromion, reproducing impingement pain.'),
    (v_topic_id, '237: A patient felt a sudden "pop" in the back of their heel while playing tennis, followed by weakness in plantarflexion. The examiner squeezes the patient''s calf muscle, and the foot fails to plantarflex. This is a positive:', '["Homan''s sign", "Thompson test", "Anterior drawer test", "Talar tilt test"]', 1, 'The Thompson test (calf squeeze test) is diagnostic for an Achilles tendon rupture. Normally, squeezing the calf causes passive plantarflexion.'),
    (v_topic_id, '238: The Apprehension test of the shoulder (abduction and external rotation with anterior pressure) is used to evaluate for:', '["Rotator cuff tear", "Biceps tendinitis", "Anterior glenohumeral instability (history of dislocation)", "Impingement syndrome"]', 2, 'This maneuver simulates the mechanism of anterior shoulder dislocation. A patient with instability will physically resist or report a feeling that the shoulder is about to "pop out."'),
    (v_topic_id, '239: During examination of the knee, the examiner applies varus stress (pushing outward from the medial side of the knee) while stabilizing the ankle. This tests the integrity of the:', '["Medial collateral ligament (MCL)", "Lateral collateral ligament (LCL)", "Anterior cruciate ligament (ACL)", "Posterior cruciate ligament (PCL)"]', 1, 'Varus stress tests the LCL, while valgus stress (pushing inward from the lateral side) tests the MCL.'),
    (v_topic_id, '240: The anterior drawer test of the knee evaluates the ACL. A false-negative result can occur if which other structure is injured and the tibia sags backward before the test begins?', '["The MCL", "The LCL", "The PCL", "The medial meniscus"]', 2, 'If the PCL is torn, the tibia "sags" posteriorly (posterior sag sign). Pulling it forward during the anterior drawer may only bring it back to a neutral position, hiding an ACL tear.'),
    (v_topic_id, '241: Which dermatome corresponds to the sensory distribution of the umbilicus?', '["T4", "T10", "L1", "L4"]', 1, 'The T10 dermatome supplies the level of the umbilicus (Remember: "T-ten at the belly buT-ten").'),
    (v_topic_id, '242: Which dermatome corresponds to the sensory distribution of the nipples?', '["T4", "T10", "C8", "L1"]', 0, 'The T4 dermatome supplies the level of the nipples (Remember: "T-four at the pore").'),
    (v_topic_id, '243: The C6 dermatome provides sensation primarily to the:', '["Thumb and lateral forearm", "Middle finger", "Little finger and medial forearm", "Shoulder tip"]', 0, 'C6 covers the thumb ("six-shooter"). C7 covers the middle fingers, and C8 covers the little finger.'),
    (v_topic_id, '244: The L4 dermatome provides sensation primarily to the:', '["Anterior thigh", "Medial lower leg and medial malleolus", "Lateral lower leg and dorsum of foot", "Lateral aspect of the foot and little toe"]', 1, 'L4 covers the medial aspect of the leg to the medial malleolus ("L4 to the floor"). L5 covers the dorsum of the foot, and S1 covers the lateral foot/pinky toe.'),
    (v_topic_id, '245: Sensation to the perineal and perianal area ("saddle area") is provided by which nerve roots?', '["L1-L2", "L3-L4", "L5-S1", "S2-S4"]', 3, 'S2, S3, and S4 supply the perineum. Loss of sensation here ("saddle anesthesia") is a red flag for Cauda Equina Syndrome.'),
    (v_topic_id, '246: A patient with cauda equina syndrome typically presents with saddle anesthesia, severe low back pain, and which other critical physical finding?', '["Hyperreflexia of the legs", "Spastic paralysis", "Bowel or bladder dysfunction (e.g., urinary retention or incontinence)", "Positive Babinski reflex"]', 2, 'The cauda equina involves lower motor neurons and parasympathetic roots (S2-S4) controlling the bladder and bowel sphincter. Loss of tone leads to retention and overflow incontinence.'),
    (v_topic_id, '247: The cremasteric reflex evaluates spinal levels L1-L2. The anal wink reflex (contraction of the anal sphincter when the perianal skin is stroked) evaluates which spinal levels?', '["T10-T12", "L3-L4", "S2-S4", "Coccygeal nerve"]', 2, 'The anal reflex tests the pudendal nerve, originating from S2-S4.'),
    (v_topic_id, '248: When testing reflexes, striking the Achilles tendon normally elicits plantarflexion of the foot. This evaluates which nerve root?', '["L3", "L4", "L5", "S1"]', 3, 'The Achilles (ankle jerk) reflex tests the S1 nerve root.'),
    (v_topic_id, '249: A patient has an absent triceps reflex, weakness in elbow extension, and numbness in the middle finger. Which cervical nerve root is most likely compressed?', '["C5", "C6", "C7", "C8"]', 2, 'C7 controls the triceps reflex, elbow extension, wrist flexion, and provides sensation to the middle finger.'),
    (v_topic_id, '250: A patient has an absent biceps reflex, weakness in elbow flexion, and numbness in the thumb. Which cervical nerve root is most likely compressed?', '["C5", "C6", "C7", "C8"]', 1, 'C6 controls the biceps/brachioradialis reflexes, elbow flexion, wrist extension, and sensation to the thumb.'),
    (v_topic_id, '251: When performing the physical exam on a newborn, the examiner notes that the urethral meatus is located on the ventral (underside) of the penis rather than at the tip. This is called:', '["Epispadias", "Hypospadias", "Phimosis", "Paraphimosis"]', 1, 'Hypospadias is a congenital defect due to failure of the urethral folds to fuse ventrally. Circumcision is contraindicated as the foreskin may be needed for surgical repair.'),
    (v_topic_id, '252: A patient presents with a tight prepuce (foreskin) that cannot be retracted over the glans penis. This condition is termed:', '["Paraphimosis", "Phimosis", "Balanitis", "Priapism"]', 1, 'Phimosis is the inability to retract the foreskin. It can be physiologic in infants but pathological if acquired via scarring.'),
    (v_topic_id, '253: A patient has retracted their tight foreskin, and now it is trapped behind the coronal sulcus, causing venous engorgement, swelling, and severe pain of the glans. This surgical emergency is:', '["Phimosis", "Paraphimosis", "Fournier gangrene", "Peyronie disease"]', 1, 'Paraphimosis is an emergency because the constricting band of foreskin can cause ischemic necrosis of the glans penis.'),
    (v_topic_id, '254: Palpation of the penile shaft reveals hard, palpable, fibrous plaques on the dorsal surface, causing the penis to curve painfully during an erection. This is characteristic of:', '["Peyronie disease", "Penile cancer", "Syphilitic chancre", "Chancroid"]', 0, 'Peyronie disease is characterized by localized fibrotic plaques in the tunica albuginea.'),
    (v_topic_id, '255: A patient has a painless, indurated, clean-based ulcer on the penis. Bilateral, rubbery, non-tender inguinal lymphadenopathy is present. This lesion is most likely a:', '["Chancroid (Haemophilus ducreyi)", "Herpes simplex virus ulcer", "Primary syphilitic chancre", "Condyloma acuminatum"]', 2, 'A primary chancre of Treponema pallidum is classically a single, painless, indurated ulcer with a clean base.'),
    (v_topic_id, '256: Conversely, a patient presents with multiple, painful, deep, purulent ulcers on the genitals with soft, tender, fluctuant inguinal lymph nodes (buboes). This is most likely:', '["Primary syphilis", "Chancroid", "Condyloma latum", "Granuloma inguinale"]', 1, 'Chancroid (caused by Haemophilus ducreyi) classically presents as painful genital ulcers with painful lymphadenopathy.'),
    (v_topic_id, '257: Condylomata acuminata (genital warts) are caused by which pathogen?', '["Herpes Simplex Virus (HSV)", "Treponema pallidum", "Human Papillomavirus (HPV)", "Neisseria gonorrhoeae"]', 2, 'Genital warts are caused by HPV, most commonly types 6 and 11.'),
    (v_topic_id, '258: During a speculum exam, the cervix has a "strawberry" appearance (punctate hemorrhages). The patient has a frothy, yellow-green, malodorous discharge. Diagnosis?', '["Bacterial vaginosis", "Vulvovaginal candidiasis", "Trichomoniasis", "Chlamydia cervicitis"]', 2, 'A "strawberry cervix" and frothy green discharge are the classic, highly specific signs of Trichomonas vaginalis infection.'),
    (v_topic_id, '259: A patient complains of vaginal discharge. Exam reveals a thin, homogenous, grayish-white discharge with a strong "fishy" odor. The vaginal pH is > 4.5. Diagnosis?', '["Bacterial vaginosis", "Vulvovaginal candidiasis", "Trichomoniasis", "Gonorrhea"]', 0, 'Bacterial vaginosis (Gardnerella vaginalis overgrowth) is diagnosed by Amsel criteria: thin discharge, clue cells, pH > 4.5, and a positive "whiff" test (fishy odor).'),
    (v_topic_id, '260: A patient has thick, white, "cottage cheese-like" vaginal discharge, severe pruritus, and vulvar erythema. The vaginal pH is normal (4.0). Diagnosis?', '["Bacterial vaginosis", "Trichomoniasis", "Vulvovaginal candidiasis", "Atrophic vaginitis"]', 2, 'Candidiasis classically presents with cottage cheese discharge and severe itching, without altering the normal acidic pH of the vagina.'),
    (v_topic_id, '261: In a pregnant patient, the fundal height (measured in cm from the pubic symphysis to the top of the uterus) typically matches the gestational age in weeks between which weeks of pregnancy?', '["10 to 16 weeks", "20 to 36 weeks", "36 to 40 weeks", "Fundal height never matches gestational age"]', 1, 'From roughly 20 to 36 weeks, the fundal height in cm roughly equals the weeks of gestation. At 20 weeks, the fundus is typically at the umbilicus.'),
    (v_topic_id, '262: The Leopold maneuvers are performed during the third trimester to determine:', '["Fetal heart rate", "Fetal lie, presentation, and position", "Amniotic fluid volume", "Placental location"]', 1, 'Leopold maneuvers involve systematic palpation of the maternal abdomen to determine if the fetus is head-down (cephalic), breech, or transverse.'),
    (v_topic_id, '263: A bluish-purple discoloration of the cervix and vaginal mucosa observed during an early pelvic exam is a sign of pregnancy known as:', '["Hegar sign", "Goodell sign", "Chadwick sign", "Chvostek sign"]', 2, 'Chadwick''s sign occurs due to increased pelvic vascularity and congestion at 6-8 weeks of gestation.'),
    (v_topic_id, '264: Softening of the cervical isthmus (the lower uterine segment) palpated during a bimanual exam in early pregnancy is known as:', '["Hegar sign", "Goodell sign", "Chadwick sign", "Homans sign"]', 0, 'Hegar''s sign is the softening of the uterine isthmus, allowing the examiner''s internal and external fingers to almost meet during bimanual exam.'),
    (v_topic_id, '265: Normal baseline fetal heart rate, as assessed by a Doppler during physical exam, is between:', '["60 and 100 bpm", "110 and 160 bpm", "160 and 200 bpm", "200 and 240 bpm"]', 1, 'The normal fetal heart rate is considerably faster than an adult''s, ranging from 110 to 160 beats per minute.'),
    (v_topic_id, '266: When evaluating a skin lesion, what does a positive "Nikolsky sign" mean?', '["The lesion turns white when pressed with a glass slide", "The top layers of the skin slip off or separate with slight lateral pressure", "The lesion bleeds profusely when the scale is removed", "The skin becomes intensely itchy when stroked"]', 1, 'A positive Nikolsky sign indicates poor intercellular adherence in the epidermis, seen in severe blistering disorders like Pemphigus Vulgaris or Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (TEN).'),
    (v_topic_id, '267: Pinpoint bleeding that occurs when a scale is scraped off a psoriatic plaque is known as:', '["Nikolsky sign", "Auspitz sign", "Koebner phenomenon", "Darier sign"]', 1, 'Auspitz sign occurs because the dermal papillae, which contain capillaries, are elongated and very close to the surface in psoriasis.'),
    (v_topic_id, '268: The development of new skin lesions (e.g., in psoriasis or lichen planus) along lines of trauma or scratching is called:', '["Nikolsky sign", "Auspitz sign", "Koebner phenomenon", "Hutchinson sign"]', 2, 'The Koebner phenomenon is the appearance of new lesions in areas of minor skin trauma.'),
    (v_topic_id, '269: A patient with a rash on their nose and cheeks (butterfly distribution) also has scaling in the concha of the ear. Which diagnosis is highly likely?', '["Rosacea", "Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (SLE)", "Seborrheic dermatitis", "Erysipelas"]', 1, 'A malar rash that spares the nasolabial folds but involves the concha of the ear is highly specific for SLE. Rosacea typically involves the nasolabial folds and lacks systemic symptoms.'),
    (v_topic_id, '270: A patient presents with a rapidly expanding, sharply demarcated, bright red, warm, and tender plaque on the face. The patient has a high fever. This is characteristic of:', '["Contact dermatitis", "Erysipelas", "Acne vulgaris", "Psoriasis"]', 1, 'Erysipelas is a superficial skin infection (usually Group A Strep) characterized by well-demarcated, raised, inflamed borders, distinguishing it from deeper, less demarcated cellulitis.'),
    (v_topic_id, '271: A patient has "target" or "bulls-eye" shaped lesions on the palms and soles. This is the hallmark physical finding of:', '["Erythema nodosum", "Erythema marginatum", "Erythema migrans", "Erythema multiforme"]', 3, 'Target lesions on the extremities are classic for Erythema multiforme, often triggered by HSV or medications.'),
    (v_topic_id, '272: A single, expanding "bulls-eye" lesion with central clearing (often at the site of a tick bite) is called:', '["Erythema nodosum", "Erythema marginatum", "Erythema migrans", "Erythema multiforme"]', 2, 'Erythema migrans is the pathognomonic early clinical sign of Lyme disease (Borrelia burgdorferi).'),
    (v_topic_id, '273: Tender, erythematous, nodular lesions located on the anterior shins are characteristic of:', '["Erythema nodosum", "Erythema marginatum", "Erythema migrans", "Pyoderma gangrenosum"]', 0, 'Erythema nodosum is an inflammation of the subcutaneous fat (panniculitis) often associated with strep infections, sarcoidosis, or IBD.'),
    (v_topic_id, '274: A patient has dark, purplish, non-blanching macules and patches on the skin, often in older adults on sun-exposed areas like the forearms. This is termed:', '["Petechiae", "Actinic keratosis", "Senile purpura", "Melanoma"]', 2, 'Senile (actinic) purpura results from atrophy of the dermal collagen supporting the fragile superficial blood vessels in the elderly, causing easy bruising.'),
    (v_topic_id, '275: When percussing the skull of a child with hydrocephalus or increased ICP, a "cracked pot" sound may be elicited. This is called:', '["Macewen sign", "Kernig sign", "Chvostek sign", "Trousseau sign"]', 0, 'The Macewen sign is a resonant sound elicited by percussion over a dilated lateral ventricle or separated cranial sutures in an infant/child.'),
    (v_topic_id, '276: A patient with hypocalcemia presents with twitching of the facial muscles when the examiner taps over the facial nerve in front of the ear. This is:', '["Trousseau sign", "Chvostek sign", "Bell''s sign", "Hoffmann sign"]', 1, 'Chvostek''s sign demonstrates neuromuscular irritability secondary to hypocalcemia.'),
    (v_topic_id, '277: Inflating a blood pressure cuff above systolic pressure for 3 minutes causes carpal spasm in a patient. This is:', '["Trousseau sign", "Chvostek sign", "Phalen sign", "Tinel sign"]', 0, 'Trousseau''s sign of latent tetany is a highly specific indicator of hypocalcemia, causing the hand to adopt an "obstetrician''s hand" posture.'),
    (v_topic_id, '278: During an eye exam, a dendritic (branching) ulcer on the cornea seen after fluorescein staining is pathognomonic for:', '["Bacterial keratitis", "Herpes simplex keratitis", "Acanthamoeba keratitis", "Corneal abrasion"]', 1, 'A dendritic ulcer is the classic sign of an active Herpes Simplex Virus infection of the cornea.'),
    (v_topic_id, '279: "Cherry-red spot" on the macula with a pale surrounding retina on fundoscopy is the classic presentation of:', '["Central retinal vein occlusion", "Central retinal artery occlusion", "Diabetic retinopathy", "Retinitis pigmentosa"]', 1, 'The retina becomes pale due to ischemia, but the fovea (supplied by the underlying choroid) remains red, creating the "cherry-red spot" contrast.'),
    (v_topic_id, '280: "Blood and thunder" retina (extensive retinal hemorrhages, dilated tortuous veins, and cotton-wool spots) is characteristic of:', '["Central retinal vein occlusion", "Central retinal artery occlusion", "Macular degeneration", "Retinal detachment"]', 0, 'CRVO causes severe venous stasis, leading to massive bursting of the retinal veins and extensive hemorrhages.'),
    (v_topic_id, '281: When evaluating the JVP, the examiner notices that the JVP paradoxically RISES during inspiration instead of falling. This is known as:', '["Kussmaul sign", "Pulsus paradoxus", "Hepatojugular reflux", "Cannon A wave"]', 0, 'Kussmaul''s sign indicates impaired right ventricular filling (e.g., constrictive pericarditis, restrictive cardiomyopathy, or severe right heart failure). Normally, inspiration decreases intrathoracic pressure, sucking blood into the chest and lowering the JVP.'),
    (v_topic_id, '282: Applying sustained pressure (10-15 seconds) over the liver in the right upper quadrant causes a sustained elevation of the JVP by >3 cm. This indicates:', '["Normal physiology", "Positive hepatojugular reflux (Abdominojugular test)", "Portal hypertension", "Tricuspid stenosis"]', 1, 'A positive hepatojugular reflux indicates that the right heart cannot accommodate the increased venous return from the compressed abdominal veins, pointing to right heart failure.'),
    (v_topic_id, '283: A loud, harsh, holosystolic murmur at the left lower sternal border that is accompanied by a palpable thrill in an otherwise healthy newborn is most likely:', '["Atrial septal defect", "Ventricular septal defect (VSD)", "Patent ductus arteriosus", "Innocent Still''s murmur"]', 1, 'Small VSDs create very loud, harsh, holosystolic murmurs due to the high pressure gradient forcing blood through a small hole.'),
    (v_topic_id, '284: An innocent, musical, vibratory, low-pitched systolic murmur heard best at the lower left sternal border in a young child (age 3-7) is a:', '["Still''s murmur", "Venous hum", "VSD murmur", "Pulmonary flow murmur"]', 0, 'Still''s murmur is the most common innocent murmur of childhood. It typically decreases in intensity with sitting or standing.'),
    (v_topic_id, '285: A continuous, low-pitched roaring or humming murmur heard at the right supraclavicular area that completely disappears when the patient lies flat or when the jugular vein is compressed is a:', '["Patent ductus arteriosus", "Venous hum", "Carotid bruit", "Aortic regurgitation"]', 1, 'A cervical venous hum is an innocent murmur caused by turbulence in the jugular veins; it is easily abolished by maneuvers that alter venous return.'),
    (v_topic_id, '286: A patient with marfanoid habitus presents with a pectus excavatum. What is a pectus excavatum?', '["A barrel-shaped chest", "A prominent, outward protrusion of the sternum (pigeon breast)", "A depressed, funnel-shaped sternum", "An asymmetric rib cage"]', 2, 'Pectus excavatum is a congenital deformity where the sternum is sunken inward. Pectus carinatum is an outward protrusion.'),
    (v_topic_id, '287: Examination of the fingernails reveals small, pinpoint depressions in the nail plate. This finding ("nail pitting") is most strongly associated with:', '["Iron deficiency", "Psoriasis", "Fungal infection (Onychomycosis)", "Endocarditis"]', 1, 'Nail pitting is a classic sign of psoriasis and is often present in patients with psoriatic arthritis.'),
    (v_topic_id, '288: "Spoon-shaped" nails (koilonychia) are a physical exam finding associated with:', '["Severe iron deficiency anemia", "Cirrhosis", "Hypoalbuminemia", "Chronic hypoxia"]', 0, 'Koilonychia describes nails that are concave like a spoon, classically seen in chronic, severe iron deficiency.'),
    (v_topic_id, '289: Small, linear, dark red streaks under the fingernails (splinter hemorrhages) combined with a new heart murmur are highly suspicious for:', '["Rheumatoid arthritis", "Infective endocarditis", "Systemic lupus erythematosus", "Scurvy"]', 1, 'Splinter hemorrhages are microemboli or vasculitic lesions under the nail bed, classic peripheral stigmata of infective endocarditis.'),
    (v_topic_id, '290: Painless, erythematous macules on the palms and soles of a patient with endocarditis are called:', '["Osler nodes", "Janeway lesions", "Roth spots", "Gottron papules"]', 1, 'Janeway lesions are painless vascular phenomena (microabscesses). Osler nodes are painful, immune-complex mediated nodules on the finger/toe pads.'),
    (v_topic_id, '291: "Roth spots" are endocarditis-related lesions found where on physical exam?', '["On the palms", "On the soles", "In the retina on fundoscopy", "Under the fingernails"]', 2, 'Roth spots are white-centered retinal hemorrhages seen on fundoscopy in patients with endocarditis.'),
    (v_topic_id, '292: A patient has violaceous, flat-topped papules over the knuckles and PIP joints. This physical finding (Gottron papules) is pathognomonic for:', '["Rheumatoid arthritis", "Systemic lupus erythematosus", "Dermatomyositis", "Psoriasis"]', 2, 'Gottron papules, along with a heliotrope rash (purple rash over the eyelids), are the classic cutaneous signs of dermatomyositis.'),
    (v_topic_id, '293: The examiner assesses a patient''s visual fields. A lesion completely transecting the right optic nerve will result in:', '["Right homonymous hemianopsia", "Bitemporal hemianopsia", "Total blindness of the right eye", "Left homonymous hemianopsia"]', 2, 'Cutting the optic nerve completely disconnects that eye from the brain, resulting in complete anopsia (monocular blindness) of that eye.'),
    (v_topic_id, '294: The presence of a "pill-rolling" resting tremor, cogwheel rigidity, and bradykinesia are the cardinal physical signs of:', '["Essential tremor", "Huntington''s disease", "Parkinson''s disease", "Multiple sclerosis"]', 2, 'These three findings (plus postural instability) form the classic tetrad of Parkinson''s disease.'),
    (v_topic_id, '295: A patient is asked to close their eyes while the examiner forcibly tries to open them. This tests the strength of the orbicularis oculi muscle, innervated by:', '["CN III (Oculomotor)", "CN V (Trigeminal)", "CN VII (Facial)", "CN IX (Glossopharyngeal)"]', 2, 'The facial nerve (CN VII) closes the eye. The oculomotor nerve (CN III) opens the eye (levator palpebrae).'),
    (v_topic_id, '296: An elderly patient complains of jaw claudication (pain with chewing) and temporal headaches. The examiner palpates the temporal artery and finds it is thickened, tender, and pulseless. This is a medical emergency due to the risk of:', '["Stroke", "Permanent, sudden blindness", "Myocardial infarction", "Ruptured aneurysm"]', 1, 'Temporal (giant cell) arteritis can quickly cause irreversible ischemic optic neuropathy leading to permanent blindness if not treated immediately with high-dose steroids.'),
    (v_topic_id, '297: A patient has unilateral pupil dilation. When a light is shone into either eye, the dilated pupil does not constrict. The patient also has ptosis and the eye looks "down and out." Which mechanism explains the dilated pupil?', '["Sympathetic overactivity", "Loss of parasympathetic fibers running on the outside of CN III", "Damage to the optic nerve (CN II)", "Horner syndrome"]', 1, 'A compressive lesion (like an aneurysm) pushing on CN III first hits the parasympathetic fibers located on the periphery of the nerve, causing loss of pupillary constriction (resulting in a blown/dilated pupil).'),
    (v_topic_id, '298: Which sign is elicited by deep palpation of the left lower quadrant causing pain in the right lower quadrant, indicating appendicitis?', '["Psoas sign", "Obturator sign", "Rovsing sign", "Murphy sign"]', 2, 'Rovsing sign involves referred rebound tenderness across the peritoneal cavity.'),
    (v_topic_id, '299: A patient with appendicitis has increased pain when their right thigh is passively extended against resistance while lying on their left side. This is a positive:', '["Psoas sign", "Obturator sign", "Rovsing sign", "Iliopsoas test"]', 0, 'The psoas sign indicates the inflamed appendix is retrocecal, resting against the psoas muscle. Extension stretches the muscle, causing pain.'),
    (v_topic_id, '300: Flexing the patient''s right hip and knee, followed by internal rotation of the hip, causes right lower quadrant pain. This is a positive:', '["Psoas sign", "Obturator sign", "Rovsing sign", "McBurney sign"]', 1, 'The obturator sign indicates the inflamed appendix is located deep in the pelvis, resting against the obturator internus muscle.');
